"""
`<wbr>` was not a break opportunity, so a word carrying one never wrapped.

`<wbr>` is a zero-width SOFT break opportunity: HTML gives it no glyph and no
advance, and CSS Text lets a line break there — but only when the content that
follows does not fit the remainder of the current line. Because `<wbr>` splits
the DOM text into separate `#text` siblings, the opportunity lands exactly at
that node and needs no intra-run machinery; the block loop in
`simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.spl` simply treated it as an ordinary
childless inline, gave it a 1 px placeholder advance, and never broke.

With `overflow-wrap: normal` (the initial value) a word wider than the line
OVERFLOWS rather than being cut, so without the `<wbr>` arm the whole word
stayed on one line and the box came out exactly one line short.

This was the largest ACTIONABLE attributed root of the 8-page Chrome parity
catalog at round 25's baseline: `html.html` `li` 86 is
`<p style="width:80px">LongWord<wbr>BreakHere</p>`, Chrome 48 against Simple
24, and that 24 px shortfall propagated into the page's `ul`, `section` and
`body` rows (~120 attributed Σ).

Every number below is HARVESTED from Chrome 152 `--headless=new
--window-size=900,20000` over byte-identical markup, not predicted:

| case                                            | Chrome h | Simple before |
|-------------------------------------------------|----------|---------------|
| `w80  LongWord<wbr>BreakHere`                    | 48       | 24            |
| `w80  LongWordBreakHere`          (control)      | 24       | 24            |
| `w80  ab<wbr>cd`                  (control)      | 24       | 24            |
| `w400 LongWord<wbr>BreakHere`     (control)      | 24       | 24            |
| `w80  Long<wbr>Word<wbr>BreakHere`               | 48       | 24            |
| `w80  Some words here now`        (control)      | 72       | 72            |

The controls carry the argument, and the fifth row is the one that makes the
rule GREEDY rather than eager. `Long<wbr>Word<wbr>BreakHere` in an 80 px box is
**two** lines, not three: Chrome takes only the opportunity it needs, so the
first `<wbr>` (after which `Word` still fits) is passed over. A predicted value
of 72 for that row was written down before the harvest and was WRONG; the
harvest is what settles it, and breaking at every `<wbr>` is the sabotage arm
this spec exists to fail.

Known residual, stated rather than hidden: a `<wbr>` at pen 0 —
`<p style="width:80px"><wbr>LongWordBreakHere</p>` — is 48 in Chrome, which
appears to open an empty first line. The implementation guards the arm with
`inline_x > inline_start_x`, so Simple answers 24 there. That degenerate case
is not exercised by any catalog page and is deliberately not encoded here.
"""

"""
Traceability: REQ-BROWSER-ENGINE-LAYOUT-017 — `<wbr>` is a zero-width soft break opportunity that is taken only when the following word does not fit the line remainder.
"""
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.spl 1%
use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer.{
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id
}

val VW: i32 = 900
val VH: i32 = 20000
val HEAD: text = "<html><head><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font:16px/1.5 sans-serif}</style></head><body>"

fn probe(inner: text, id: text) -> text:
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id(HEAD + inner + "</body></html>", VW, VH, id, "h")

describe "<wbr> is a soft break opportunity taken only when the next word does not fit":
    it "AC-1: the catalog's LongWord<wbr>BreakHere box is 48, not 24":
        step("Chrome 48 = two 24 px line boxes; this is html.html li 86")
        expect(probe("<p id=\"o\" style=\"width:80px\">LongWord<wbr>BreakHere</p>", "o")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-2: a greedy breaker uses only the opportunity it needs":
        step("Chrome 48, NOT 72 — Long+Word still fit on line one, so the first <wbr> is passed over")
        step("this row fails the moment the arm breaks at every <wbr> unconditionally")
        expect(probe("<p id=\"o\" style=\"width:80px\">Long<wbr>Word<wbr>BreakHere</p>", "o")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-3 (control): the SAME word without a <wbr> still overflows one line":
        step("Chrome 24 — overflow-wrap:normal forbids an intra-word cut, so nothing may break it")
        expect(probe("<p id=\"o\" style=\"width:80px\">LongWordBreakHere</p>", "o")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-4 (control): a <wbr> whose following word FITS adds no line":
        step("Chrome 24 — the opportunity exists but is not needed, and <wbr> adds no width")
        expect(probe("<p id=\"o\" style=\"width:80px\">ab<wbr>cd</p>", "o")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-5 (control): the same markup in a wide box stays on one line":
        step("Chrome 24 at width 400 — the arm must be conditional on the line remainder, not on the tag")
        expect(probe("<p id=\"o\" style=\"width:400px\">LongWord<wbr>BreakHere</p>", "o")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-6 (control): ordinary space wrapping in the same box is untouched":
        step("Chrome 72 = three lines; the <wbr> arm must not disturb the normal breaker")
        expect(probe("<div id=\"o\" style=\"width:80px\">Some words here now</div>", "o")).to_equal("72")
